## STEP 5 — NEXT ACTION

**[NEXT] AUTH_HELPED**: With an authorized api.sparelabs.com session (Bearer token in `Authorization`), run `curl -s -H "Origin: https://evil.example.com" -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>" -H "Accept: application/json" https://api.sparelabs.com/v1/global/organizations?id=<valid-org-uuid>` and `curl -s -H "Origin: https://evil.example.com" -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>" https://api.us.sparelabs.com/v1/global/organizations?id=<valid-org-uuid>` to verify whether (a) the fail-open returns non-empty per-tenant data under authenticated scope (proving data-leak primitive, not just `{"data":[]}`), and (b) `access-control-allow-origin:<reflected>` + `access-control-allow-credentials:true` present on 200 response (proving malicious-origin exfiltration of victim's authenticated tenant data). Control: confirm `/v1/journeys` still 401 with credentials reflected. This closes the gap between confirmed CORS misconfiguration and real-world exploitability. A real valid organization UUID is needed (no UUIDs leaked in platform/forms JS bundles — only zero UUID placeholder found).

